- How does this change if moves include taking from both ends with a coin flip to decide who starts?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to derive a tree DP with multiple subtrees and an exact count constraint. Requires knapsack-style merging across children and careful reconstruction to report all k endpoints. Also checks edge-disjointness handling and correctness when k exceeds leaves.

## Key Concepts

- Tree DP with per-node knapsack merge
- Exact-path counting and edge-disjointness
- Result reconstruction: path endpoints
- Complexity reasoning: O(n k^2) time, O(n k) space

## Code Example

```javascript
function dfs(u, k, val, children, dp) {
  let f = Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity);
  f[0] = 0;
  for (const v of children[u]) {
    let g = Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity);
    for (let i = 0; i <= k; i++) {
      if (f[i] <= -Infinity) continue;
      for (let t = 0; t + i <= k; t++) {
        g[i+t] = Math.max(g[i+t], f[i] + dp[v][t]);
      }
    }
    f = g;
  }
  for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) dp[u][t] = f[t] + t * val[u];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you modify the DP if paths could start at any node but must end at a leaf? 
- How would you adapt reconstruction to output the exact endpoints efficiently?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP with a spacing constraint and path reconstruction, a common interview variation.

## Key Concepts
- DP over prefix with constrained subsequences
- 2D state and reconstruction pointers
- Handling infeasible states and space-time trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
// state definitions: dp[i][t], par[i][t]
function solve(A, n, k, d) {
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const par = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for (let i=1; i<=n; i++) dp[i][1] = A[i-1];
  for (let t=2; t<=k; t++) {
    for (let i=1; i<=n; i++) {
      // skip i
      if (dp[i-1][t] > dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t]; par[i][t] = -1; }
      // take i
      if (i - d >= 1 && dp[i-d][t-1] !== NEG) {
        const val = dp[i-d][t-1] + A[i-1];
        if (val > dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = val; par[i][t] = i-d; }
      }
    }
  }
  return {dp, par};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you reconstruct the indices from par to return the actual solution?
- How can you optimize space without changing asymptotic time?

## Why This Is Asked

This problem tests the ability to formulate a DP solution over prefixes with a partition constraint, combining range-based computations (maximum values) with block length considerations. It requires clear state definition, proper transition logic, and a strategy for reconstructing the solution from computed results.

## Key Concepts

- Dynamic programming over array prefixes and block count parameters
- State transitions focusing on the last block contribution
- Efficient handling of range maximum queries during transitions
- Solution reconstruction using parent pointers or backtracking
- Time-space trade-offs and potential RMQ-based optimizations

## Code Example

```javascript
// DP framework structure
function partitionArray(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const dp = Array(n+1).fill().map(() => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array(n+1).fill().map(() => Array(k+1).fill(-1));

  // Base cases: single block
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    const maxVal = Math.max(...A.slice(0, i));
    dp[i][1] = maxVal * i;
  }

  // Fill DP table
  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = t; i <= n; i++) {
      for (let j = t-1; j < i; j++) {
        const maxLast = Math.max(...A.slice(j, i));
        const val = dp[j][t-1] + maxLast * (i-j);
        if (val > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = val;
          par[i][t] = j;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct blocks
  const blocks = [];
  let curr = n, blocksLeft = k;
  while (blocksLeft > 0) {
    const start = par[curr][blocksLeft];
    blocks.push([start, curr-1]);
    curr = start;
    blocksLeft--;
  }

  return { value: dp[n][k], blocks: blocks.reverse() };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you optimize the range maximum queries to achieve O(1) time per query?
- What memory optimizations can reduce the space complexity from O(nk)?
- How would the solution change if the blocks could be non-contiguous?
- Can you propose a divide-and-conquer optimization to improve the time complexity?

## Why This Is Asked
New angle: exactly k non-overlapping subarrays maximize sum, a knapsack-like DP variant with efficient inner maximization. It mirrors budgeted promotion planning.

## Key Concepts
- DP state: dp[i][t] is the best total for first i elements using t blocks
- Prefix sums P enable O(1) subarray sums
- Optimize inner max with best = max(dp[j-1][t-1]-P[j-1]) up to i
- Reconstruction via pointers to endpoints

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(a,k){
  const n=a.length;
  const P=[0];
  for(let v of a) P.push(P[P.length-1]+v);
  const dp=Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  dp[0].fill(0);
  for(let t=1;t<=k;t++){
    let best=-Infinity;
    for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
      dp[i][t]=dp[i-1][t];
      best=Math.max(best, dp[i-1][t-1]-P[i-1]);
      const cand=P[i]+best;
      if(cand>dp[i][t]) dp[i][t]=cand;
    }
  }
  return dp[n][k];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for at-most-k subarrays?
- How would you recover the exact endpoints from the DP table?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests deriving a clean DP where subarray cost depends on endpoints, not internal aggregates, and requires careful index handling and backtracking.

## Key Concepts
- DP state dp[i][t] for first i elements into t blocks
- Transition over split point p with endpoint cost |A[p+1]-A[i]| 
- Backpointer reconstruction for endpoints
- Time O(n^2 k), space O(nk) (can optimize with rolling arrays)

## Code Example
```javascript
function minEndPointPartition(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) dp[i][1] = Math.abs(A[0] - A[i]);
  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = t - 1; i < n; i++) {
      for (let p = t - 2; p <= i - 1; p++) {
        const val = dp[p][t - 1] + Math.abs(A[p + 1] - A[i]);
        if (val < dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = val; par[i][t] = p;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  let endpoints = [];
  let i = n - 1, t = k;
  while (t > 0) {
    const p = par[i][t];
    endpoints.unshift([p + 1, i]);
    i = p; t--;
  }
  return { cost: dp[n - 1][k], endpoints };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt if cost were |A[l]-A[r]|^2 or involved a different function?
- Can you optimize to O(nk) time with monotone queue tricks if the cost has structure?

## Why This Is Asked

This question introduces a non-linear block value that combines sum and minimum, forcing a DP that accounts for subarray metrics and partition points. It also tests reconstruction of exact k segments and handling of large intermediate data.

## Key Concepts

- Non-linear block cost: (sum) * (min)
- Precomputation of all subarray metrics V[l][r]
- DP over prefixes with exactly k blocks
- Path reconstruction from predecessor pointers
- Time/space trade-offs: O(n^2 k) time, O(n^2) space (reducible to O(nk))

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k){
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++) pref[i+1] = pref[i] + A[i];
  const V = Array.from({length:n}, ()=> Array(n).fill(0));
  for(let l=0;l<n;l++){
    let mn = Infinity;
    for(let r=l;r<n;r++){
      mn = Math.min(mn, A[r]);
      const sum = pref[r+1]-pref[l];
      V[l][r] = sum * mn;
    }
  }
  const NEG = -1e18;
  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const pre = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++) dp[i][0] = NEG;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=1;t<=k;t++){
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      pre[i][t] = pre[i-1][t];
      for(let j=0;j<i;j++){
        const val = dp[j][t-1] + V[j][i-1];
        if(val > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = val; pre[i][t] = j; }
      }
    }
  }
  let blocks = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while(t>0){
    const j = pre[i][t];
    blocks.push([j, i-1]);
    i = j; t--;
  }
  blocks.reverse();
  return { value: dp[n][k], blocks };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you reduce memory to O(nk) with a streaming recomputation of V? 
- How would the approach adapt if k is large and n up to 2e5 (any pruning or heuristic)?

## Why This Is Asked
Evaluates DP on selecting exact number of non-overlapping subarrays with reconstruction, common in profit/segmentation tasks.

## Key Concepts
- Non-overlapping subarrays
- Exact k selections
- Prefix sums for efficient range sums
- Backpointers for reconstruction

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k){
  const n = A.length; const pre = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++) pre[i] = pre[i-1]+A[i-1];
  const neg inf = -1e18;
  let dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> new Array(k+1).fill(neg_inf));
  let best = new Array(k+1).fill(neg_inf);
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for(let t=1;t<=k;t++) best[t] = neg_inf;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=1;t<=k;t++){
      best[t] = Math.max(best[t], dp[i-1][t-1] - pre[i-1]);
      dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i-1][t], pre[i] + best[t]);
    }
  }
  // reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return dp[n][k];
}
```

